1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,600
Let me quickly explain you what we're gonna do

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:06,600
when we are setting up our tables and users and everything in Snowflake

3
00:00:06,600 --> 00:00:08,598
Now as of November 2025

4
00:00:08,598 --> 00:00:11,596
Snowflake made quite some changes

5
00:00:11,596 --> 00:00:14,594
in how you can authenticate into it

6
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:19,100
So one is a username and password based authentication

7
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:23,000
You can have a multi factor app on your device

8
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,369
That works well but it doesn't work for so called service account

9
00:00:27,369 --> 00:00:31,995
A service account is a non human entity accessing Snowflake

10
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:34,018
For example our dbt project

11
00:00:34,018 --> 00:00:37,477
It's not a human it's a piece of software

12
00:00:37,477 --> 00:00:39,783
so that is a service account

13
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,563
which means that we won't be able to use

14
00:00:42,563 --> 00:00:45,789
a single username and password authentication for that

15
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:48,300
The second one is that you can generate

16
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,050
a personal access token in Snowflake

17
00:00:51,050 --> 00:00:54,800
so a random string basically and authenticate with that

18
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:59,735
But in order to use that you will need to set up network policies in Snowflake

19
00:00:59,735 --> 00:01:02,790
So that's more like an enterprise grade setting

20
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,322
Then there is single sign on

21
00:01:05,322 --> 00:01:09,738
Single sign on means that you integrate Snowflake

22
00:01:09,738 --> 00:01:14,784
with your Google workspace or Office 365 suite or whatever

23
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,854
and you go through these providers

24
00:01:16,854 --> 00:01:19,113
That's something we don't want to do

25
00:01:19,113 --> 00:01:21,784
because we don't want to overcomplicate things

26
00:01:21,800 --> 00:01:24,000
And the fourth option and as you can see

27
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,800
this is what we're going to use

28
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:27,800
is the key pair based authentication

29
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:32,859
Now I want to show you how the key pair based authentication works

30
00:01:32,859 --> 00:01:36,793
so you have a basic idea about what we're doing

31
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:38,482
Disclaimer I love this topic

32
00:01:38,482 --> 00:01:40,796
I could speak about this for hours

33
00:01:40,800 --> 00:01:44,487
This is what fuels digital signatures and digital certificates

34
00:01:44,487 --> 00:01:46,791
and HTTPS and everything around it

35
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,925
So if you are an analytics engineer or a data engineer

36
00:01:50,925 --> 00:01:53,950
I would suggest you to go and look up

37
00:01:53,950 --> 00:01:57,800
the public key authentication or PKI infrastructure

38
00:01:57,800 --> 00:02:03,791
to understand how this whole encryption and communication works It's super useful

39
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:06,220
But here comes a one minute intro

40
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:08,640
You as a user will create and generate

41
00:02:08,640 --> 00:02:11,786
with a piece of software a so called key pair

42
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:16,203
A key pair consists of a private key that you never give anyone

43
00:02:16,203 --> 00:02:18,793
and the public key which is public

44
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,796
So you can just give it to someone if they require it

45
00:02:21,800 --> 00:02:25,862
Now these two keys are mathematically interconnected

46
00:02:25,862 --> 00:02:30,792
which means that if you encrypt a message with the public key

47
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:38,792
then it will be only the private key holder who can decrypt this message That's the main thing

48
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:42,790
So what we're going to do is we're going to generate our key pair for us

49
00:02:42,800 --> 00:02:46,584
We will register our public key into Snowflake

50
00:02:46,584 --> 00:02:48,790
keep our private key with us

51
00:02:48,800 --> 00:02:50,708
and then when we want to authenticate

52
00:02:50,708 --> 00:02:53,464
Snowflake will be able to come up with a message

53
00:02:53,464 --> 00:02:55,796
encrypt it with the public key we provided

54
00:02:55,800 --> 00:02:58,251
send the encrypted message to us

55
00:02:58,251 --> 00:03:00,972
and then we will be able to decode it

56
00:03:00,972 --> 00:03:05,795
decrypt it thus proving Snowflake that we are the actual key holders

57
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:10,177
Now in reality the whole thing is somewhat more sophisticated than that

58
00:03:10,177 --> 00:03:11,789
but you get the intuition

59
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:15,800
So we're going to use our private key in our dbt project

60
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:17,800
and also later in our BI tool

61
00:03:17,800 --> 00:03:20,050
And with the help of these two keys

62
00:03:20,050 --> 00:03:23,800
we will be able to authenticate and connect to Snowflake

63
